<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Phagmodrupa dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">1354 CE – 1618 CE · capital Nedong</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The dynasty that replaced the Mongol-backed Sakya and restored native rule over central Tibet, patrons of the rising Gelug school.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changchub-gyaltsen">Changchub Gyaltsen</a>, myriarch of Phagmodru, a domain linked to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagyu">Kagyu</a> monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/densatil">Densatil</a>, fought the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sakya-regime">Sakya regime</a>'s administrators from the 1340s. By 1354 he controlled most of central <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet</a> and ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nedong">Nedong</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarlung-valley">Yarlung</a> valley.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changchub-gyaltsen">Changchub Gyaltsen</a> reorganised the country into districts governed by appointed officials from fortresses (dzong), issued a law code inspired by the age of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/tibetan-empire">Tibetan Empire</a>, and encouraged a revival of imperial traditions. His successors, called gongma, received titles from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drakpa-gyaltsen-phagmodrupa">Drakpa Gyaltsen</a> supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsongkhapa">Tsongkhapa</a>, who founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganden-monastery">Ganden</a> monastery in 1409, the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> school.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After a succession crisis in 1434, real power passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/rinpungpa">Rinpungpa</a> ministers and later to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tsangpa-dynasty">Tsangpa dynasty</a>. The Phagmodrupa rulers survived as nominal kings at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nedong">Nedong</a> until the early 17th century.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/phagmodrupa-dynasty.jpg" alt="Phagmodrupa dynasty" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: DamiKarv · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phagmodrupa_dynasty.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1354 CE – 1618 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nedong</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Changchub Gyaltsen, Drakpa Gyaltsen, Ngawang Tashi Drakpa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tibetan Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tibetan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Phagmodru, Phagmodrupa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1486&amp;focus=phagmodrupa-dynasty" aria-label="Phagmodrupa dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7073 1108 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Phagmodrupa dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7393 1364L7643 1242M7393 1364L7317 1364"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7643 1242h0M7317 1364h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7393 1364h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7393 1364h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Nedong. <a href="/atlas?year=1486&amp;focus=phagmodrupa-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
